Calculate the entropy change (J/mol K) of the given reaction. The molar entropies [J/K mol] are given in brackets after each substance.

\[ 2 \text{PbS(s)} [91.2] + 3 \text{O}_2(g) [205.1] \rightarrow 2 \text{PbO(s)} [66.5] + 2 \text{SO}_2(g) [248.2] \]
</USER_REQUEST>

<ASSISTANT_RESPONSE>
The entropy change (\(\Delta S\)) for the reaction is calculated using the formula:
\[
\Delta S = \sum \left( n \times \Delta S^\circ \text{ (products)} \right) - \sum \left( n \times \Delta S^\circ \text{ (reactants) \right)
\]
where \( n \) is the stoichiometric coefficient of each reactant or product.

Substituting the given molar entropies:
\[
\Delta S = \left( 2 \times 66.5 \right) + \left( 2 \times 248.2 \right) - \left( 2 \times 91.2 \right) - \left( 3 \times 205.1 \right)
\]
\[
\Delta S = 132.0 + 496.4 - 182.4 - 615.3
\]
\[
\Delta S = 628.4 - 797.7 = -169.3 \text{ J/mol K}
\]

Thus, the entropy change for the reaction is \(\boxed{-169.3 \text{ J/mol K}}\).
</ASSISTANT_RESPONSE>

