</USER_REQUEST>

<ASSISTANT_RESPONSE>
The potential of the cell is calculated using the Nernst equation, which is given by:
\[
\text{E} = \text{E}_{\text{anode}} - \text{E}_{\text{anode}}
\]
The standard reduction potentials are:
\[
\text{E}_{\text{Cu}^{2+}} = 0.342 \, \text{V}
\]
\[
\text{E}_{\text{Ag}^{2+}} = 0.7996 \, \text{V}
\]
The half-reactions are:
\[
\text{Cu}^{2+} + \text{e}^- \rightarrow \text{Cu} \quad \text{(reduction)}
\]
\[
\text{Ag}^{2+} + \text{e}^- \rightarrow \text{Ag} \quad \text{(reduction)}
\]
The standard cell potential is:
\[
\text{E}_{\text{std}} = \text{E}_{\text{anode}} - \text{E}_{\text{cathode}} = 0.7996 \, \text{V} - 0.342 \, \text{V} = 0.4576 \, \text{V}
\]
The Nernst equation is:
\[
\text{E} = \text{E}_{\text{std}} - \frac{RT}{F} \ln \frac{\text{Ag}^{2+} \text{H}^2 \text{H}^2}}{(\text{Cu}^{2+})}
\]
Assuming standard conditions (25°C), the gas constant \( R = 8.314 \, \text{J/mol·K} \), the temperature \( T = 298 \, \text{K} \), and the Faraday's constant \( F = 96485 \, \text{C/mol} \). The Nernss equation becomes:
\[
\text{E} = \text{E}_{\text{std}} - \frac{0.02918}{96485} \times 298 \ln \frac{0.1}{0.2}
\]
\[
\text{E} = 0.4576 - 0.00822 \ln \frac{0.1}{0.2}
\]
\[
\text{E} = 0.4576 - 0.00822 \ln 0.5
\]
\[
\text{E} = 0.4576 - 0.00822 \times (-0.301)
\]
\[
\text{E} = 0.4576 + 0.00248 \, \text{V}
\]
\[
\text{E} = 0.46008 \, \text{V}
\]
The potential of the cell is \(\boxed{0.46008 \, \text{V}}\).
