","To calculate the standard enthalpy change (ΔH) for the reaction, we can use the formula:

ΔH = Σ(ΔH°_f) (products) - Σ(ΔH°_f) (reactants)

where ΔH°_f is the standard enthalpy of formation for each substance.

In this case, the reaction is:

CaO(s) + H2O(l) → Ca(OH)2(s)

Given standard heats of formation:

ΔH°_f(CaO(s)) = -635.1 kJ/mol
ΔH°_f(H2O(l)) = -285.8 kJ/mol
ΔH°_f(Ca(OH)2(s)) = -986.2 kJ/mol

Now, we can calculate the standard enthalpy change:

ΔH = ΔH°_f(Ca(OH)2(s)) - [ΔH°_f(CaO(s)) + ΔH°_f(H2O(l))]

ΔH = (-986.2 kJ/mol) - (-635.1 kJ/mol + (-285.8 kJ/mol))

ΔH = -986.2 kJ/mol - (-321.9 kJ/mol)

ΔH = -986.2 kJ/mol + 321.9 kJ/mol

ΔH = -664.3 kJ/mol

The standard enthalpy change for the reaction between calcium oxide and water to form calcium hydroxide is -645.3 kJ/mol.",fluency,"# Task Description
