Since the electronegativity of oxygen (14.4) is greater than the electronegativity of carbon (12.4), the oxygen-oxygen bond is electronegative. This means that the oxygen atoms are more electronegative than the carbon atom, and they will attract the electrons in the bond more than the carbon atom. This results in the carbon-oxygen bond being **polar**.

