The probability of getting 9 heads in 12 flips is given by the binomial probability formula:
\[
\frac{1}{2}^{12} \times \binom{12}{9} = \frac{1}{2}^{12} \times \frac{12!}{9!3!} = \frac{1}{2}^{12} \times \frac{12 \times 11 \cdot 10}{3 \cdot 2 \cdot 1} = \frac{1}{2}^{12} \times 48 = \frac{48}{360} = \frac{2}{15}
\]
The probability of getting 10 heads in 12 flips is:
\[
\frac{1}{2}^{12} \times \binom{12}{10} = \frac{1}{2}^{12} \times \frac{12!}{10!2!} = \frac{1}{2}^{12} \times \frac{12 \times 11}{2} = \frac{1}{2}^{12} \times 60 = \frac{60}{360} = \frac{1}{6}
\]
The probability of getting 11 heads in 12 flips is:
\[
\frac{1}{2}^{12} \times \binom{12}{11} = \frac{1}{2}^{12} \times \frac{12!}{11!1!} = \frac{1}{2}^{12} \times 12 = \frac{12}{360} = \frac{1}{30}
\]
The probability of getting 12 heads in 12 flips is:
\[
\frac{1}{2}^{12} \times \binom{12}{12} = \frac{1}{2}^{12} \times 1 = \frac{1}{360}
\]
The probability of getting at than than heads in 12 flips is the sum of these probabilities:
\[
\frac{48}{360} + \frac{60}{360} + \frac{12}{360} + \frac{1}{360} = \frac{48 + 60 + 12 + 1}{360} = \frac{121}{360}
