<USER_REQUEST>
You’re pulling a chest of drawers, applying a force of 60.0 N at an angle of 60.0°. How much work do you do pulling it over 10.0 m?
</USER_REQUEST>

<ASSISTANT_RESPONSE>
To calculate the work done, we use the formula:

\[
W = F \cdot d \cdot \cos \theta
\]

Where:
- \( W = \text{work done} \)
- \( F = \text{force applied} \)
- \( d = \text{distance traveled} \)
- \( \theta = \text{angle of the force with the direction of displacement} \)

Substituting the given values:
- \( F = 60.0 \, \text{N} \)
- \( d = 10.0 \, \text{m} \)
- \( \theta = 60.0° \)

\[
W = 60.0 \, \text{N} \times 10.0 \, \text{m} \times \cos 60.0°
\]

\[
W = 600 \, \text{N·m} \times 0.5
\]

\[
W = 300 \, \text{J}
\]

Thus, the work done is \(\boxed{300 \, \text{J}}\).
</ASSISTANT_RESPONSE>

