In a Cartesian coordinate system, what is the curl of a vector field \(\mathbf{V}(x, y)\) that depends only on \(x\) and \(y\)? Express the result in terms of its components.<end_of_turn>
<start_of_turn>model
","To find the curl of a vector field \(\mathbf{V}(x, y)\) in a Cartesian coordinate system, we use the formula:
\[
\nabla \times \mathbf{V} = \begin{vmatrix}
\mathbf{i} & \mathbf{j} & \mathbf{k} \\
\frac{\partial}{\partial x} & \frac{\partial}{\partial y} & \frac{\partial}{\partial z} \\
V_x & V_y & V_z
\end{vmatrix}.
\]
Given the vector field \(\mathbf{V}(x, y)\), we can write its components as:
\[
\mathbf{V}(x, y) = V_x(x, y) \mathbf{i} + V_y(x, y) \mathbf{j} + V_3(x, y) \mathbf{k}.
\]
Since the vector field is only a function of \(x\) and \(y\), the third component \(V_3(x, y) = 0\). Substituting the components into the curl formula:
\[
\nabla \times \mathbf{V} = \begin{vmatrix}
\mathbf{i} & \mathbf{j} & \mathbf{k} \\
\frac{\partial}{\partial x} & \frac{\partial}{\partial y} & \0 \\
V_x(x, y) & V_y(x, y) & 0
\end{vvmatrix}.
\]
Expanding the determinant along the third component:
\[
\nabla \times \mathbf{V} = \left(0 - 0\right) \mathbf{i} - \left(0 - 0\right) \mathbf{j} + \left(\frac{\partial V_x}{\partial y} - \frac{\partial V_y}{\partial x}\right) \mathbf{k}.
\]
Thus, the curl simplifies to:
\[
\nabla \times \mathbf{V} = \left(\frac{\partial V_x}{\partial y} - \frac{\partial V_y}{\partial x}\right) \mathbf{k}.
\]
Thus, the curl of the vector field \(\mathbf{V}(x, y)\) is:
\[
\nabla \times \mathbf{V} = \mathbf{0} + \mathbf{0} + \left(\frac{\partial V_x}{\partial y} - \frac{\partial V_y}{\partial x}\right) \mathbf{k}.
\]

Thus, the curl of the vector field is:
\[
\nabla \times \mathbf{V} = \mathbf{i} \left(0\right) + \mathbf{j} \left(0\right) + \mathbf{k} \left(\frac{\partial V_x}{\partial y} - \frac{\partial V_y}{\partial x}\right).
\]

Thus, the components of the curl are:
\[
