   E = \frac{3}{2} P V \left( \gamma - \1 \right)
   \]  
   where \( E = 1.0 \text{ L} \times 10^5 \text{ Pa} \times 10^5 \text{ Pa} \times 1.4 - 1 \), \( P = 1.0 \text{ kPa} = 10^5 \text{ Pa} \), \( V = 1.0 \text{ L} = 0.001 \text{ m}^3 \), and \( \gamma = 1.4 \).  
   Substituting the values:  
   \[
   E = \frac{3}{2} \times 10^5 \text{ Pa} \cdot 0.001 \text{ m}^3 \times (1.4 - 1) = 0.15 \text{ kJ} = 150 \text{ kJ}
   \]  

2. **Calculate the energy in the pressure wave**:  
   The energy in the pressure wave is 70% of the total energy:  
   \[
   \text{Energy in pressure wave} = 0.7 \times 150 \text{ kJ} = 105 \text{ kJ} = 105,000 \text{ J}
   \]  

3. **Calculate the grams of TNT required**:  
   The energy released by 1 g of TNT is 4680 J.  
   The grams of TNT required to release the same energy is:  
   \[
   \text{grams of TNT} = \frac{\text{Energy in pressure wave}}{\text{Energy per gram of TNT}} = \frac{105,000 \text{ J}}{4680 \text{ J/g}} \approx 22.2 \text{ g}
   \]  

**Final Answer**:  
