","The lifetime of a particle is related to its decay width \(\Gamma\) by the following formula:

\[
\Gamma = \frac{h}{2\tau},
\]

where \(h\) is the Planck constant (\(6.626 \times 10^{-34} \text{ J} \cdot \text{s}\)) and \(\tau\) is the lifetime.

The decay width is related to the rest energy \(\gamma\) by:

\[
\Gamma = \frac{2\pi^2 c^2}{3\hbar} \gamma,
\]

where \(c\) is the speed of light (\(3 \times 10^8 \text{ m/s}\)) and \hbar = \frac{hbar}{2\pi} \approx 1.017 \times 10^{-34} \text{ J} \cdot \text{s}\).

Substituting the first equation into the second:

\[
\frac{h}{2\tau} = \frac{2\pi^2 c^2}{3\hbar} \gamma,
\]

Solving for \(\gamma\):

\[
